reviewText:
This is a bit of a weird combination. The Xbee shield is not usable because it doesn't come with the xbee module itself. I guess I wasn't paying close enough attention when I ordered it. The LCD shield is wired "differently" to the ones expected by the Arduino examples. In order to run the examples from Arduino, you'd need to change the following line in the examples:
LiquidCrystal lcd(12, 11, 5, 4, 3, 2);

to:
LiquidCrystal lcd(8,9,4,5,6,7);

Works well, although your PC's BIOS may blacklist it (refuse to turn the WiFi radio on).

If this is the case, you should try masking off pin 13; any thin non-conductive tape should work.  Be careful to ONLY mask pin 13 and not any of the adjoining pins.

To find pin 13, hold the card so that the pins, (contacts) are facing you and the key (a little cut in the card) in on the left.  Pins are counted up-then-down and after that left-to-right:

{{{
1  key  3  5  7  9  11 13 15 ...
2  key  4  6  8 10 12 14 16 ...
}}}

Pin 13 will be thus the seventh pin from the left edge (or the sixth pin from the key) on the upper side of the card.

Once you have found pin 13, mask it with a piece of tape, reinstall the card and test.
